preConditionHook:
// The gap window has ELAPSED: no gap bar printed in the last withinBars bars,
// so it is no longer 'too early to tell'. Brooks's point is the block -- for
// a while after a fresh breakout gap you cannot know whether it is a
// breakaway gap or a measuring gap, so do not fade it -- and the book says
// the distinction is retrospective. The rule is written in the PASSING sense
// and the per-placement NEGATE toggle gives the blocking sense, which is how
// the corpus states every 'do not trade while X' claim.
//
// 'barsSince(gap) > withinBars' is mechanized as its exact contrapositive: no
// gap bar at offsets 0..withinBars. That also covers what the book leaves
// implicit -- a chart with no gap at all passes, bars-since being unbounded.
//
// The gap primitive is TrueGapBar's relation, the whole-range non-overlap
// with the previous bar, and BOTH directions count: a fresh gap either way
// starts the window. Sides are therefore identical text.
//
// The loop runs WITHOUT a break and the flag is cleared rather than the scan
// abandoned -- EasyLanguage has no loop break, and Max Bars Back must cover
// withinBars + 1 on every bar regardless. noGap is a class member and
// persists across bars, so it is set at the top rather than relied on to
// start true. The comparisons carry the EL relational tolerance (el_gt /
// el_lt), as TrueGapBar's do.
noGap = true;
for (kk = 0; kk <= (int)withinBars; ++kk) {
    if (el_gt(low[kk], high[kk + 1])) noGap = false;
    if (el_lt(high[kk], low[kk + 1])) noGap = false;
}
